WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.063 --> 00:00:01.420
- I was talking on the
phone with my brother,

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.272
who's stationed in Fort Bragg,

00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:05.808
and they have a 9:00 p.m. curfew.

00:00:05.808 --> 00:00:08.420
What on earth is a 9:00 p.m. curfew doing

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.660
doing to stop the spread of a pandemic?

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.893
Bars are already closed.

00:00:11.893 --> 00:00:13.533
This doesn't make any sense.

00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:14.636
This is just something

00:00:14.636 --> 00:00:19.636
for simple-minded people to
express how much they did

00:00:20.913 --> 00:00:22.540
to combat the pandemic.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:23.760
You didn't do anything.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.407
You infringed on people's
freedoms for no reason.

00:00:25.407 --> 00:00:26.938
You have to have a good reason

00:00:26.938 --> 00:00:28.970
for implementing the restrictions

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.242
that people are gonna implement.

00:00:30.242 --> 00:00:33.242
(suspenseful music)

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.150
- I'm Dave Rubin and
this is The Rubin Report.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.137
Reminder, guys, you can get
all of our episodes days early

00:00:44.137 --> 00:00:46.388
and totally ad-free at rubinreport.com.

00:00:46.388 --> 00:00:49.130
And joining me today
is a former Navy SEAL.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.780
The congressman from Texas'
second district and author

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.780
of "Fortitude: American
Resilience in the Era of Outrage",

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.370
Congressman Dan Crenshaw.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.021
Finally, welcome to The Rubin Report.

00:01:01.021 --> 00:01:02.686
- Great to be with you, Dave.

00:01:02.686 --> 00:01:05.185
- We've been trying to pull
this off for a long time,

00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:10.185
but, I guess, it took a
pandemic for us to accept Skype

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:11.801
as the only way we could make it happen.

00:01:11.801 --> 00:01:14.010
- (laughs) I know, that's true.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.830
I was supposed to come
to LA for my book tour,

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:17.663
as you know,

00:01:17.663 --> 00:01:18.740
we had some events planned together,

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.767
but life happens.

00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:22.670
- Life happens.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:23.621
Indeed it does.

00:01:23.621 --> 00:01:25.680
All right, well, let's
start with the stuff

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.640
that we gotta get out of
the way no matter what

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:28.730
before we get into the book,

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:29.830
and your story, and everything else,

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:33.654
which is just coronavirus,
sorta where we're at.

00:01:33.654 --> 00:01:35.236
I'm really curious for your thoughts

00:01:35.236 --> 00:01:36.937
on the stimulus package

00:01:36.937 --> 00:01:39.921
and the way we watched it sort of unfold.

00:01:39.921 --> 00:01:42.493
But just, generally, can you
talk about what it's like

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:44.730
to be in Congress right now,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:48.313
in a time of truly
unprecedented happenings?

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.213
- Yeah, it's odd in the same way

00:01:51.213 --> 00:01:53.230
that it's probably odd for everybody else.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.562
You're trying to continue doing your job,

00:01:55.562 --> 00:01:58.000
hopefully you still have your job,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.300
but you're trying to do it from home.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.570
You're trying to be as
effective as you were before,

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.712
but you're doing it from home,

00:02:04.712 --> 00:02:07.004
and that's difficult.

00:02:07.004 --> 00:02:09.706
More psychologically than logistically.

00:02:09.706 --> 00:02:11.470
We can make it happen logistically.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.573
Our office is still operating as normal.

00:02:14.407 --> 00:02:19.407
The frustrating thing about
how this is played out,

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.797
from the congressional standpoint,

00:02:20.797 --> 00:02:24.444
is how the partisan
games didn't really stop,

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:27.523
and whereas we maybe thought they would.

00:02:27.523 --> 00:02:30.063
I think I had some hope that they would,

00:02:30.063 --> 00:02:32.972
mostly because they were
stopping to an extent.

00:02:32.972 --> 00:02:35.927
I'll never forget Chuck Schumer on T.V.

00:02:35.927 --> 00:02:37.908
that Saturday in late February,

00:02:37.908 --> 00:02:41.940
talking about how bipartisan
this rescue package was going,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.070
and it was going well.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:47.383
Good talks, he said, "Surprisingly
good, bipartisan talks."

00:02:47.383 --> 00:02:49.910
And then, it all blew up the next day,

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:51.730
and he quickly shifted tax.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.487
And went right along with Pelosi's demands

00:02:55.487 --> 00:02:58.464
of this crazy, progressive wishlist.

00:02:58.464 --> 00:03:02.440
Ultimately, the package
went through days later,

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.116
after millions of jobs had been lost.

00:03:04.116 --> 00:03:05.660
But, ultimately, went through,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.250
and it was basically the same package,

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.550
and anybody who argues otherwise

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.986
is being totally intellectually dishonest.

00:03:10.986 --> 00:03:12.103
The changes were minor

00:03:12.103 --> 00:03:14.404
and could have been agreed
to that weekend prior.

00:03:14.404 --> 00:03:16.080
That was extremely disappointing.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.010
That was also a turning point.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:20.221
I think we all noticed a
turning point right then

00:03:20.221 --> 00:03:22.770
that came from the left-wing media.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.500
And that turning point
was Trump is to blame

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.902
for millions of deaths,
or thousands, right?

00:03:26.902 --> 00:03:31.707
That was then the new narrative,
and it hasn't stopped.

00:03:31.707 --> 00:03:35.840
In fact, and it shifts slightly, okay.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.017
You saw it shift from saying,

00:03:40.017 --> 00:03:42.357
"Hey, Trump's travel restrictions

00:03:42.357 --> 00:03:44.550
"from China weren't really
travel restrictions."

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.700
There was, "He could have done more."

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.380
All right, well, that's a
dishonest talking point,

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.010
because the reality is the people coming

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.646
from China after January
31st were American citizens

00:03:51.646 --> 00:03:52.977
or green card holders.

00:03:52.977 --> 00:03:54.690
So, now you're gonna have to explain

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.931
to me why we shouldn't be
repatriating our own citizens?

00:03:56.931 --> 00:04:00.450
Okay, these are just really
disingenuous remarks.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.442
Now, the new talking point is

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:04.920
that they didn't do anything in February.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.554
Okay, fine, so the travel
restriction was good.

00:04:06.554 --> 00:04:07.657
We agree with that.

00:04:07.657 --> 00:04:09.211
Even though we didn't at the time,

00:04:09.211 --> 00:04:11.010
and even though the
Democrats were in charge,

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.216
they never would have put
those travel restrictions in.

00:04:13.216 --> 00:04:16.047
Fine, but Trump didn't
do anything in February,

00:04:16.047 --> 00:04:18.679
except for deny the whole thing.

00:04:18.679 --> 00:04:21.763
Well, again, is this really
an honest talking point?

00:04:23.009 --> 00:04:25.550
With 2020 hindsight,
could we have done more?

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.847
Yes. But with the facts
that we had at the time,

00:04:27.847 --> 00:04:31.921
I question what exactly you
think they should have done.

00:04:31.921 --> 00:04:34.990
More PPE production and
ventilator production,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.164
more testing production?

00:04:36.164 --> 00:04:37.986
I agree with all that.

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:40.560
I just don't think it's reasonable

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.387
to be looking back in time and saying,

00:04:42.387 --> 00:04:43.947
"You made this decision differently

00:04:43.947 --> 00:04:45.256
"than I would have made this decision."

00:04:45.256 --> 00:04:46.662
'Cause that's just not true.

00:04:46.662 --> 00:04:48.590
- Is that the toughest part

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.420
of doing what you guys have to do,

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.924
in terms of preparing
for something like this?

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:53.001
'Cause, right now,

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:54.648
the hindsight thing is
just driving me crazy

00:04:54.648 --> 00:04:56.440
when you hear it from the media.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.187
That oh, suddenly, they're saying,

00:04:58.187 --> 00:05:01.130
"Everybody knew this
was coming in February."

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:02.588
There were, I think, three,

00:05:02.588 --> 00:05:05.980
possibly four, democratic
debates in February.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.160
The last one in February, I
believe, was February 25th.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.510
Coronavirus was not
mentioned in any of them.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:14.050
It wasn't mentioned until the one

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:17.150
where they only had Biden
and Bernie in a room alone

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:18.180
because of coronavirus.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.280
That was already into March.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.987
So, you see everybody, it's like,

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:23.286
"Now that we see the score of the game,

00:05:23.286 --> 00:05:25.913
we can tell you everything
that you did wrong before."

00:05:25.913 --> 00:05:30.395
But that's not really how you
can prepare as a government.

00:05:30.395 --> 00:05:31.714
- 100% true.

00:05:31.714 --> 00:05:35.482
I'm about to release a
video on this exact thing.

00:05:35.482 --> 00:05:37.960
I did a podcast on this,

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.820
I think it was a week and a half ago,

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:42.984
where I really, I do
30 minutes of timeline

00:05:42.984 --> 00:05:46.067
on exactly who was
saying what at what time.

00:05:46.067 --> 00:05:48.660
I'm not trying to make
excuses for Trump here.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:50.284
I just want people to
understand the truth,

00:05:50.284 --> 00:05:52.870
and you made another good point.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:54.397
I didn't even bring up
that point in my timeline.

00:05:54.397 --> 00:05:56.505
They weren't talking about this,

00:05:56.505 --> 00:05:58.480
nobody was talking about this.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:01.179
In late February, Pelosi and
De Blasio were telling people

00:06:01.179 --> 00:06:03.170
to go celebrate the Chinese New Year.

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.203
Go out, don't be afraid.

00:06:04.203 --> 00:06:05.729
And I'm not blaming them,

00:06:05.729 --> 00:06:08.600
they had the same set of
facts that we all did.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:09.980
I don't think they're responsible

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:11.050
for people's deaths either.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.481
I think this is a bad situation

00:06:13.481 --> 00:06:15.570
that we didn't quite expect.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:16.403
I also point out,

00:06:16.403 --> 00:06:19.430
You know in Italy, they
didn't shut down their society

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:20.263
until March 10th?

00:06:20.263 --> 00:06:22.285
Spain, not until March 14th.

00:06:22.285 --> 00:06:26.332
That's way later than
February, as it turns out.

00:06:26.332 --> 00:06:30.240
We did our 15 days to slow
the spread on March 16th.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.420
Iran didn't even start until April 4th.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.942
Sweden, still hasn't started
and probably never will.

00:06:36.942 --> 00:06:40.090
These are really disingenuous attacks,

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:41.650
and as it pertains to something like PPE,

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.280
even with hindsight,

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.196
it's not obvious that the
PPE shortage was going

00:06:46.196 --> 00:06:47.164
to be there.

00:06:47.164 --> 00:06:48.894
I think people simplify

00:06:48.894 --> 00:06:51.875
what information government
officials truly have

00:06:51.875 --> 00:06:54.620
and are capable of having.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:59.140
It's not like there's this
final tally of N95 masks

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:00.710
and the government just
knows what they are

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:01.730
and knows what they'll need.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:03.000
Well, no, I mean,

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.290
hospitals are also
responsible for stockpiling.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.520
In Houston, we're well-stockpiled.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.712
Maybe, because we've been
through pandemics before.

00:07:10.712 --> 00:07:12.420
We've been through hurricanes before,

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:15.276
and our hospital system operates
a little bit differently

00:07:15.276 --> 00:07:17.240
than maybe another hospital system

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:20.056
that doesn't feel they
need a bunch of N95 masks.

00:07:20.056 --> 00:07:22.520
That's not necessarily the
federal government's fault.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.470
Then again, if we really point fingers,

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:25.750
if we really wanna go down this road,

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:27.685
I will point you to all the fact-checkers

00:07:27.685 --> 00:07:29.349
that have confirmed

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:30.853
that it's really the Obama administration

00:07:30.853 --> 00:07:33.174
that didn't replenish
our national stockpile

00:07:33.174 --> 00:07:37.036
of N95 masks after the H1N1 virus.

00:07:37.036 --> 00:07:39.250
But should I really blame them?

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:40.260
I mean, that's another question.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.656
Should I really be blaming
the Obama administration?

00:07:42.656 --> 00:07:44.430
What crystal ball do they have?

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:45.388
- Right, we can always

00:07:45.388 --> 00:07:46.413
blame somebody.
- They spent that money

00:07:46.413 --> 00:07:47.246
on different things.

00:07:47.246 --> 00:07:49.650
They spent it on radiological protection

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:50.700
and things like that.

00:07:51.696 --> 00:07:54.257
There's no ill-intent here.

00:07:54.257 --> 00:07:56.290
And I will also point out

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:59.370
that our hospital system's
have been strained

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:00.203
but not broken.

00:08:00.203 --> 00:08:02.118
Nobody's been denied a ventilator,

00:08:02.118 --> 00:08:03.616
unlike in places in Europe.

00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:04.510
Why?

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:05.343
Because, well,

00:08:05.343 --> 00:08:08.594
turns out our profit-driven
healthcare system has an excess

00:08:08.594 --> 00:08:10.750
of things like ventilators and ICU beds.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:13.154
I went through the numbers on this.

00:08:13.154 --> 00:08:17.967
On a per capita basis,
the US has way more,

00:08:17.967 --> 00:08:19.550
orders of magnitude more,

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:22.310
ICU beds and ventilators than any

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.540
of these western countries
or countries in Europe.

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.760
Any of them, including Germany.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.550
And that's a product of
our healthcare system.

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:32.010
It's an imperfect healthcare system.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.917
I wish it was different in a lot of ways.

00:08:33.917 --> 00:08:37.980
But socialize medicine means less supply,

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.120
because you're basically
setting price controls.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:41.980
And people need to
understand these basic facts

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:43.937
as we judge what could have been done

00:08:43.937 --> 00:08:45.890
and what should have been done.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.330
- So, speaking of what could've been done,

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:48.163
what should've been done,

00:08:48.163 --> 00:08:49.500
can you just explain a little bit

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.765
about how information
actually gets to you guys?

00:08:53.765 --> 00:08:55.925
How does it get to a
member of Congress that,

00:08:55.925 --> 00:08:59.249
say, in January, there's
sort of these initial reports

00:08:59.249 --> 00:09:00.407
of something going on.

00:09:00.407 --> 00:09:03.840
Trump does the initial shut down,

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:05.828
I think it was on last
day of January, I think.

00:09:05.828 --> 00:09:08.404
But when does something
actually get to your desk

00:09:08.404 --> 00:09:10.463
and how do you actually decide,

00:09:10.463 --> 00:09:13.924
oh, now this is something I
gotta talk to somebody about.

00:09:13.924 --> 00:09:16.407
Or, do you pass it on to somebody and say,

00:09:16.407 --> 00:09:17.770
"Does this study make sense?"

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:19.724
Or, "Is anyone else seeing these reports?"

00:09:19.724 --> 00:09:22.376
How does some of that
information actually turn

00:09:22.376 --> 00:09:23.705
into something actionable?

00:09:23.705 --> 00:09:26.133
- That's an interesting question.

00:09:27.142 --> 00:09:31.430
We don't have a whole lot of
secret lines of information

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:32.570
that the public doesn't have.

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:35.480
Oftentimes, members of
Congress are really operating

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.465
off of the same set of facts

00:09:37.465 --> 00:09:40.229
that most people in the media are

00:09:40.229 --> 00:09:43.372
or most members of the public.

00:09:43.372 --> 00:09:46.842
The difference is we have
sets of professionals

00:09:46.842 --> 00:09:47.991
that we can rely on

00:09:47.991 --> 00:09:51.306
to sift through all of the information

00:09:51.306 --> 00:09:54.701
and place that against prior policy,

00:09:54.701 --> 00:09:56.724
compare that against prior legislation,

00:09:56.724 --> 00:10:00.163
and then talk about it
in legislative terms.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:03.760
I just have a team that helps me do that,

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.785
so that I'm not a journalist
trying to do it all myself.

00:10:07.785 --> 00:10:09.740
And so, that's a huge advantage

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.856
as far as sifting through the information

00:10:11.856 --> 00:10:13.719
and making sure that we get it correct.

00:10:13.719 --> 00:10:16.000
As far as what you do
with that information,

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.000
that can be wildly different
for every member of Congress.

00:10:21.226 --> 00:10:24.490
Every member of Congress is
representing their district

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:27.480
and as such they tend
to then have priorities,

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:28.313
and things that they think

00:10:28.313 --> 00:10:30.983
that should be maybe more
important than other things.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:33.881
And so, 'cause in theory,

00:10:33.881 --> 00:10:36.981
in theory, a given member
of Congress could do nothing

00:10:36.981 --> 00:10:37.994
for two years.

00:10:37.994 --> 00:10:41.750
There's not some obligatory schedule

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:43.336
that anybody is on, right.

00:10:43.336 --> 00:10:45.504
In theory, they don't even have to vote.

00:10:45.504 --> 00:10:47.490
And I'm sure their
constituents wouldn't like

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:49.254
that very much, and they
would vote them out of office

00:10:49.254 --> 00:10:50.615
in two years.

00:10:50.615 --> 00:10:53.980
But it's really up to you.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:56.460
I mean, you've got a lot of
freedom as an elected member

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.855
to prioritize and make
things happen accordingly.

00:10:59.855 --> 00:11:03.289
And information comes to us, again,

00:11:03.289 --> 00:11:06.706
it can come directly from
The White House, of course,

00:11:06.706 --> 00:11:09.250
especially being a Republican.

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:11.079
If it was a Democrat White House,

00:11:11.079 --> 00:11:13.960
I would hope that we would
still see those kind of things.

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:15.890
I know my Democrat
colleagues still get a lot

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:17.083
of updates as well.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:21.976
But it's different for every issue.

00:11:21.976 --> 00:11:24.310
So, that's why I'm kind of fumbling

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:25.303
that question a little bit,

00:11:25.303 --> 00:11:27.190
because there isn't a given set of rules

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:30.620
that dictate how that information flows.

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:33.940
It's relationships, it's
you watching the media,

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:35.897
it's you seeing what
other members are saying,

00:11:35.897 --> 00:11:39.307
and that's talking to
industry, to your constituents.

00:11:39.307 --> 00:11:40.921
It comes from all angles.

00:11:40.921 --> 00:11:43.040
- No, that actually does strike me

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:44.490
as a pretty clean and honest answer,

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:45.850
because I think people think it's

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:48.398
as if you guys just all
get a report in the morning

00:11:48.398 --> 00:11:49.797
and then it's like,

00:11:49.797 --> 00:11:51.228
"Oh, now, this is what we've gotta do

00:11:51.228 --> 00:11:52.870
"about this thing here."

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:54.950
But before we move on from corona though,

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:56.155
and I wanna obviously talk about the book,

00:11:56.155 --> 00:11:57.495
you mentioned the stimulus before,

00:11:57.495 --> 00:11:59.181
and can you just talk a little bit

00:11:59.181 --> 00:12:01.339
about what that process is like,

00:12:01.339 --> 00:12:05.030
and when you suddenly see
everybody on every side.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:05.863
Although, in this case,

00:12:05.863 --> 00:12:07.106
I think the most egregious examples were

00:12:07.106 --> 00:12:08.796
by the Progressives,

00:12:08.796 --> 00:12:11.425
about just all the pork
they stuff in there

00:12:11.425 --> 00:12:14.320
and all these things
that have nothing to do

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:16.940
with helping the economy
because of coronavirus.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:19.606
And then it's things
like Nancy Pelosi happens

00:12:19.606 --> 00:12:22.566
to be on the board of
the Kennedy Arts Center,

00:12:22.566 --> 00:12:25.429
and all of these things that
we all know are terrible.

00:12:25.429 --> 00:12:29.490
And when limited government
people see those things,

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:30.323
it's like, man,

00:12:30.323 --> 00:12:32.170
if you guys can't even help us now,

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:33.540
if you can't even do it right now,

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:37.230
then even the little bit
of the limited part starts

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:39.340
to become really freaking
irrelevant, you know?

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:43.096
- Yeah, as far as the process
on this particular bill,

00:12:43.096 --> 00:12:45.193
and unfortunately a lot of bills,

00:12:45.193 --> 00:12:49.393
it is a closed group of
leadership that's doing that.

00:12:49.393 --> 00:12:52.573
And that's for the sake of expediency.

00:12:54.204 --> 00:12:56.570
That doesn't mean people don't have a say.

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:58.605
There's a constant conversation

00:12:58.605 --> 00:13:01.686
and ways to get input into there.

00:13:01.686 --> 00:13:04.350
Of course, it's hard to
put input into a bill,

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:05.360
if you're not really sure

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:08.516
how the bill is being formed anyway.

00:13:08.516 --> 00:13:11.198
Our leadership does a pretty decent a job

00:13:11.198 --> 00:13:13.479
of keeping us informed in a private way.

00:13:13.479 --> 00:13:16.099
So, maybe it's conference
calls, or whatever it is,

00:13:16.099 --> 00:13:19.680
so that we can say, "Hold on,
we're not in favor of this."

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.680
Just like I was outspoken
against direct cash payments,

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:25.803
I don't think that made any
sense for a lot of reasons.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:29.583
And in the same way Progressives,

00:13:29.583 --> 00:13:31.415
and some more populous conservatives,

00:13:31.415 --> 00:13:35.609
were against any kind of help
to big businesses at all,

00:13:35.609 --> 00:13:37.000
if they're just loans.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.973
And so, people still get
their voice heard on that.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.250
But the reality is,

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:49.250
yes, a lot of this is negotiated
in closed door sessions.

00:13:49.343 --> 00:13:52.800
And I'm not so sure that's the wrong way,

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.048
because you could debate it
publicly on the House floor,

00:13:56.048 --> 00:13:57.880
I suppose, but where would we get

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:01.440
and how long would that take
when this is an emergency?

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.820
So, whether that's good or bad,

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:07.260
is, I think, truly up for debate.

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:10.043
As far as, the pork
goes, it's frustrating.

00:14:10.043 --> 00:14:14.558
You didn't see me making
a huge fuss about it.

00:14:14.558 --> 00:14:17.643
Mostly, because we're talking
about millions of dollars

00:14:17.643 --> 00:14:20.360
when we passed a $2
trillion stimulus package.

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:22.073
So, I try to look at everything

00:14:22.073 --> 00:14:23.607
through a lens of perspective.

00:14:23.607 --> 00:14:28.607
And also, I try to interpret
the best of intentions

00:14:28.885 --> 00:14:31.122
from the people who wanted that in there,

00:14:31.122 --> 00:14:34.292
which was these particular organizations,

00:14:34.292 --> 00:14:35.384
like the Kennedy Center,

00:14:35.384 --> 00:14:37.600
it will shed jobs, if we don't fund them,

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:40.484
because they make their
money off of events.

00:14:40.484 --> 00:14:41.794
Now, the frustrating part

00:14:41.794 --> 00:14:44.210
for everybody was they still shed jobs.

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:45.655
Like many businesses are.
- Like what, two days later?

00:14:45.655 --> 00:14:46.981
- Like many businesses are.

00:14:46.981 --> 00:14:51.049
This isn't something that made me angry,

00:14:51.049 --> 00:14:53.304
especially since I just wrote a whole book

00:14:53.304 --> 00:14:56.940
about (laughs) not engaging in outrage

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.903
and having some perspective,
and looking a few layers deep

00:14:59.903 --> 00:15:03.790
into why something happened as a method

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:05.910
to sort of temper your own emotions.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:09.937
So, it's generally hard to
get me emotionally upset

00:15:09.937 --> 00:15:12.355
and I don't understand it
from either side of the aisle,

00:15:12.355 --> 00:15:14.935
frankly, when people get
emotional about things.

00:15:14.935 --> 00:15:17.899
It doesn't mean they're
wrong to show concern,

00:15:17.899 --> 00:15:19.714
but I wouldn't let it
keep you up at night.

00:15:19.714 --> 00:15:21.249
- All right, so just
one more on this then.

00:15:21.249 --> 00:15:25.060
What else should the
government be doing right now?

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:27.230
I mean, we're all still in lockdown.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:29.920
Yesterday, I went to my
local hardware store,

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.769
and I saw that I could buy
seeds to plant some things,

00:15:32.769 --> 00:15:34.620
which I did buy, but I know that

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.310
in Michigan you actually
can't buy seeds right now.

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:39.615
I mean, we've got a series
of kinda crazy things.

00:15:39.615 --> 00:15:41.848
And I'm all for the governors

00:15:41.848 --> 00:15:43.870
and the states doing what they want.

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:44.959
I think, you mostly are as well,

00:15:44.959 --> 00:15:47.365
but there are some
strange things happening.

00:15:47.365 --> 00:15:49.640
But what do you think the role

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:50.473
of the federal government

00:15:50.473 --> 00:15:51.928
and the states should be right now?

00:15:51.928 --> 00:15:54.962
And are they basically
working together pretty well?

00:15:54.962 --> 00:15:58.140
- Yeah, I think the
balance is about right.

00:15:58.140 --> 00:15:59.302
I think every once in a while,

00:15:59.302 --> 00:16:01.446
even the president says
kinda the wrong thing,

00:16:01.446 --> 00:16:04.530
whether he's talking
about forcing a re-opening

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.810
or forcing a national
lockdown, neither is okay.

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:08.761
You can't do it.

00:16:08.761 --> 00:16:09.821
That's up to the states,

00:16:09.821 --> 00:16:11.386
and in states like Michigan,

00:16:11.386 --> 00:16:14.043
where the governor is
making up stupid rules,

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:15.855
they should be held accountable.

00:16:15.855 --> 00:16:17.244
I hope voters are holding,

00:16:17.244 --> 00:16:18.336
I think it's a her, right?

00:16:18.336 --> 00:16:19.390
- Yeah, it's a her.

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:23.245
- Deeply accountable for these
completely foolish policies,

00:16:23.245 --> 00:16:25.640
that are really just
based in virtue signaling,

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:30.640
not based in actual, even two
seconds of logical thought.

00:16:33.467 --> 00:16:34.870
I was talking on the
phone with my brother,

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:36.728
who's stationed in Fort Bragg,

00:16:36.728 --> 00:16:38.426
and they have a 9:00 p.m. curfew.

00:16:38.426 --> 00:16:42.006
What on earth is a 9:00 p.m. curfew doing

00:16:42.006 --> 00:16:44.130
to stop the spread of a pandemic?

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:44.971
Bars are already closed.

00:16:44.971 --> 00:16:46.678
This doesn't make any sense.

00:16:46.678 --> 00:16:51.380
This is just something
for simple-minded people

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:56.010
to express how much they
did to combat the pandemic.

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:57.210
You didn't do anything.

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:59.148
You infringed on people's
freedoms for no reason.

00:16:59.148 --> 00:17:00.431
You have to have a good reason

00:17:00.431 --> 00:17:02.128
for implementing the restrictions

00:17:02.128 --> 00:17:03.410
that people are gonna implement.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:05.600
Okay, so what's next, to your question.

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:07.680
What's next is we have

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.150
to start having a real conversation

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:10.867
about how to re-open the economy,

00:17:10.867 --> 00:17:15.867
alongside battling and reducing
the risk of the pandemic.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:19.326
And this looks a lot like a,

00:17:19.326 --> 00:17:23.025
what Harvard economists and
political scientists are calling

00:17:23.025 --> 00:17:25.161
a mobilize-and-transition phase.

00:17:25.161 --> 00:17:28.218
I know our governor here
in Texas is gonna come out

00:17:28.218 --> 00:17:29.720
with his plan soon.

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:31.325
I think the federal government is as well.

00:17:31.325 --> 00:17:33.020
I've just written a op-ed about it,

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:35.531
and it basically looks like this.

00:17:35.531 --> 00:17:40.000
Look, we have to dispel this myth

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:41.180
that there's a binary choice

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:44.260
between a perpetual lockdown
and just letting people die

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:45.730
in the spread of the pandemic.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:48.300
Neither of these things are
true, you can do both, okay.

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:49.320
And we have to do both.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:50.160
This is not an option.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:51.560
We have to do both.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:54.324
And I think along three factors

00:17:54.324 --> 00:17:56.270
that we have to be focused on.

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.590
Testing capacity, which
we're a little behind on,

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:01.550
but I think we can rather quickly ramp up

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:02.485
to the right standard.

00:18:02.485 --> 00:18:03.775
Public health capacity,

00:18:03.775 --> 00:18:05.893
meaning our hospitals,
our ventilators, our PPE.

00:18:05.893 --> 00:18:08.509
I think we're getting to a
much better place on that.

00:18:08.509 --> 00:18:12.317
And then, public education
on how to deal with it,

00:18:12.317 --> 00:18:15.121
whether that's standing six
feet away from each other,

00:18:15.121 --> 00:18:16.708
wearing a mask to work.

00:18:16.708 --> 00:18:20.185
Just making those
micro-changes in your life

00:18:20.185 --> 00:18:21.789
and in your business

00:18:21.789 --> 00:18:24.322
so that we can live alongside a pandemic

00:18:24.322 --> 00:18:27.245
for a period of time,
until there's a vaccine.

00:18:27.245 --> 00:18:28.375
That's the new normal.

00:18:28.375 --> 00:18:29.912
And it doesn't involve shutting

00:18:29.912 --> 00:18:31.228
down the economy indefinitely.

00:18:31.228 --> 00:18:32.934
This is the last thing I wanna say.

00:18:32.934 --> 00:18:35.425
I get really worried
when we become obsessive

00:18:35.425 --> 00:18:38.659
over the nature of the curve and the peak.

00:18:38.659 --> 00:18:40.580
Those are important data points,

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:41.825
but they're not the data points.

00:18:41.825 --> 00:18:43.223
There's other data points,

00:18:43.223 --> 00:18:45.514
like our sense of preparedness
along those three lines

00:18:45.514 --> 00:18:46.783
that I just mentioned.

00:18:46.783 --> 00:18:49.138
When I hear our mayor talking

00:18:49.138 --> 00:18:52.260
about how we can't open up Houston,

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:53.670
because we haven't reached the peak yet.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:54.895
I find that to be absurd,

00:18:54.895 --> 00:18:57.345
because if you put our numbers
that we have here in Houston,

00:18:57.345 --> 00:18:59.074
which is very, very small.

00:18:59.074 --> 00:19:01.230
Very, very small.

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:02.420
We're the fourth largest city in America,

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:05.840
and we barely have any cases,
comparatively speaking.

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:06.790
If you put our numbers

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:08.504
and place those in New
York City right now,

00:19:08.504 --> 00:19:10.470
New York City would be celebrating,

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:12.330
and they'd open up everything.

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:15.280
So, if that can be true,

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:17.956
then it tells you that
our logic isn't correct

00:19:17.956 --> 00:19:21.540
as we think about what our goalposts are

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:26.132
to open up the economy.

00:19:26.132 --> 00:19:29.070
And I'm encouraging leaders to think

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:30.695
through this a little bit more deeply.

00:19:30.695 --> 00:19:32.178
- It's one of the reasons actually

00:19:32.178 --> 00:19:34.660
that I'm kinda hopeful for the future

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:35.760
when we get outta this,

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:37.560
that some of the right ideas,

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:39.440
that I think you're
writing about in this book,

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:40.380
will break forth.

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:42.120
I mean, things like states' rights.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:45.130
We're actually talking about
states' rights right now

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:47.470
for the first time in this country in god,

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:49.719
I don't know, maybe since Roe v. Wade.

00:19:49.719 --> 00:19:51.720
We never talk about states' rights,

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:52.797
and suddenly people are like,

00:19:52.797 --> 00:19:54.603
"Oh, governors are supposed to do things,

00:19:54.603 --> 00:19:57.291
"and the president doesn't
have ultimate authority."

00:19:57.291 --> 00:19:59.372
And a whole bunch of other things.

00:19:59.372 --> 00:20:00.627
So, since the book is

00:20:00.627 --> 00:20:03.706
about American resilience
in the era of outrage,

00:20:03.706 --> 00:20:07.420
one thing on American
resilience related to corona,

00:20:07.420 --> 00:20:10.339
specifically, are you worried
that as this has gone on,

00:20:10.339 --> 00:20:13.730
and maybe as some of the
states get back to work,

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:14.750
and some take longer,

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:17.060
and it looks like New York
will take a really long time.

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:19.360
But just, the longer
that we're in our houses,

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:20.950
the longer that the economy is shut,

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:24.630
the longer that we kinda
sputter along, that thing,

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:26.443
the American resilience
that you write about here,

00:20:26.443 --> 00:20:28.869
that's the special
thing we have in America

00:20:28.869 --> 00:20:31.400
that every other country wishes they had.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:35.410
But the more that we sort of
just let government do things

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:36.855
and oh, oh, the peak didn't happen yet,

00:20:36.855 --> 00:20:39.740
we'll stay in our house for another month.

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:43.200
That we'd actually start
forgetting what that spirit

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:44.410
of American resilience is.

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:45.440
That's what I'm kinda,

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:48.740
I see a lot of silver
linings, as I just mentioned,

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:50.130
but that's the thing I'm worried about.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:51.042
That over time,

00:20:51.042 --> 00:20:54.130
we would just start accepting
more and more rules,

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:57.500
and accepting that more and
more experts know what to do,

00:20:57.500 --> 00:20:58.780
when perhaps they don't.

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:00.593
And then, the bad actors in government

00:21:00.593 --> 00:21:03.023
that just sorta wanna control all of us.

00:21:04.834 --> 00:21:07.901
- Those are definitely reasonable fears.

00:21:07.901 --> 00:21:09.770
I think, unfortunately, the reality is

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:13.223
is we shifted towards that
acceptance a long time ago.

00:21:17.237 --> 00:21:20.940
I think this entire
experience might bring us back

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:23.374
to normalcy to an extent,

00:21:23.374 --> 00:21:27.161
where we do see that experts
don't have all the answers.

00:21:27.161 --> 00:21:29.560
Their data is important,
their opinions are important,

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:33.260
but it takes a leader to sift through all

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:35.073
of those opinions, and
then make a bold decision,

00:21:35.073 --> 00:21:37.770
and then get people to rally

00:21:37.770 --> 00:21:40.523
behind that decision and mobilize.

00:21:41.613 --> 00:21:45.193
We are seeing some terrible
infringements on freedom.

00:21:47.104 --> 00:21:49.240
It's not so much a problem for me

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:52.120
that government is telling
you to avoid the beach.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:53.015
Well, that's silly.

00:21:53.015 --> 00:21:54.778
It doesn't make any practical sense.

00:21:54.778 --> 00:21:58.320
But my point is, I don't mind
that they're telling you that,

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:00.102
what I do mind is that
they'll arrest you for it.

00:22:00.102 --> 00:22:02.676
That's a bridge too far.

00:22:02.676 --> 00:22:05.727
And we're not dealing with that in Texas.

00:22:05.727 --> 00:22:08.820
I know you are in California
and in other places.

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:09.853
But that's not okay.

00:22:09.853 --> 00:22:11.970
I think you're gonna see
some terrible lawsuits

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:14.623
after all this is done.

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:18.301
You have to have the
lightest touch possible.

00:22:18.301 --> 00:22:22.103
The government does have a
place in ensuring public health,

00:22:22.103 --> 00:22:23.954
but with a light touch.

00:22:23.954 --> 00:22:25.844
And we're getting it wrong.

00:22:25.844 --> 00:22:28.804
In some places, people
are getting it wrong.

00:22:28.804 --> 00:22:31.380
And because there's such a temptation

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:32.870
to keep people safe, right.

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:35.980
That's what every politician
wants to say the most of.

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:38.022
And they say it because it works.

00:22:38.022 --> 00:22:42.614
And to your point, we
have to resist that urge

00:22:42.614 --> 00:22:46.510
as Americans to always
want to be kept safe,

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:48.720
to believe that it's the government's duty

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:50.860
to not just allow us to
pursue our happiness,

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:52.161
but to make us happy.

00:22:52.161 --> 00:22:54.744
And to keep us safe, at any cost.

00:22:54.744 --> 00:22:57.010
When you see this debate play out,

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:59.310
when people try to genuinely talk

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:00.382
about opening up the economy.

00:23:00.382 --> 00:23:02.868
Well, the most disingenuous
counter to that is,

00:23:02.868 --> 00:23:04.810
oh, well, how many lives is to worth

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:06.096
to you to save that job?

00:23:06.096 --> 00:23:07.507
That's not the right question.

00:23:07.507 --> 00:23:08.876
You know it's not the right question.

00:23:08.876 --> 00:23:10.970
I can flip that right back on you,

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:12.927
I can say, "Well, I'm a more moral person,

00:23:12.927 --> 00:23:15.715
"because I wanna keep
40000 people a year alive.

00:23:15.715 --> 00:23:17.147
"And you know how I'm gonna do that?

00:23:17.147 --> 00:23:18.032
"No more driving.

00:23:18.032 --> 00:23:19.655
"It's done, driving is done."

00:23:19.655 --> 00:23:22.090
Well, but that's not reasonable.

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:26.790
But I can make a completely
objective argument

00:23:26.790 --> 00:23:28.490
that I would save that many lives.

00:23:29.446 --> 00:23:30.603
And you're not moral,

00:23:30.603 --> 00:23:32.080
you're not a good person,

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:33.250
because you don't wanna agree with me.

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:34.388
Well, that's not how we live our lives.

00:23:34.388 --> 00:23:36.218
We have risks and we mitigate those risks.

00:23:36.218 --> 00:23:38.040
And it's time that we be bold enough

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:41.388
to confront those risk with
the new tools that we have.

00:23:41.388 --> 00:23:44.761
Now, the way I describe
the coronavirus issue is

00:23:44.761 --> 00:23:47.317
we've taken a tactical retreat,

00:23:47.317 --> 00:23:52.300
and in that tactical retreat,

00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:53.930
which is, in SEAL terms,

00:23:53.930 --> 00:23:55.588
means we get shot away
from the target, all right?

00:23:55.588 --> 00:23:57.234
We didn't know what hit us.

00:23:57.234 --> 00:24:00.009
We went back, we retreated a little bit.

00:24:00.009 --> 00:24:01.860
We reload, we call in some air support,

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:04.543
and then we move back
to confront the enemy.

00:24:04.543 --> 00:24:06.770
There is no perpetual retreat.

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:08.344
That would just be failure and defeat.

00:24:08.344 --> 00:24:09.177
And that's how we have
to look at this too.

00:24:09.177 --> 00:24:14.177
And I have a lot of faith
in the American people

00:24:14.218 --> 00:24:16.422
on a micro level to do that.

00:24:16.422 --> 00:24:20.090
What I'm not seeing is the ability

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:24.650
of our more public
discussions to also engage

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:26.578
in that sense of fortitude

00:24:26.578 --> 00:24:31.030
and avoid the political opportunism

00:24:31.030 --> 00:24:32.643
that we've seen thus far.

00:24:32.643 --> 00:24:35.352
- Well, listen, that's why
I've got you on right now.

00:24:35.352 --> 00:24:37.033
So, let's fix it together.

00:24:37.033 --> 00:24:38.930
So, let's go back to the beginning,

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:41.630
the thing that sort of
made you a national figure,

00:24:41.630 --> 00:24:42.980
although you were a congressman already.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.470
Let's talk about Saturday Night Live.

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:47.340
I'm sure you've never talked
about that before, right.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:48.955
Maybe once, maybe twice.

00:24:48.955 --> 00:24:50.870
Can you just talk a little bit

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:52.369
about that night,

00:24:52.369 --> 00:24:54.863
you see the Pete Davidson sketch,

00:24:54.863 --> 00:24:57.558
and then just sort of all the fall-out,

00:24:57.558 --> 00:24:59.034
and then what you did ultimately,

00:24:59.034 --> 00:25:02.659
was it literally the next week,
when you went on the show.

00:25:02.659 --> 00:25:05.770
And just talk about how
that then sorta set you up

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:07.264
to be a more national figure,

00:25:07.264 --> 00:25:10.663
instead of just a congressman from Texas.

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:13.523
- Well, obviously,

00:25:13.523 --> 00:25:16.440
I wasn't really watching
it that night, of course.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:17.273
Nobody is, right?

00:25:17.273 --> 00:25:20.651
I think most people
digest Saturday Night Live

00:25:20.651 --> 00:25:23.499
from the next day's YouTube
videos or something,

00:25:23.499 --> 00:25:25.076
if it's a good skit.

00:25:25.076 --> 00:25:27.451
And that was no different
on this one, of course.

00:25:27.451 --> 00:25:29.647
Stories started getting written about it.

00:25:29.647 --> 00:25:31.530
I had not seen it,

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:33.890
I had some funny text
messages from my buddies

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:36.170
that were just giving
me a hard time for it.

00:25:36.170 --> 00:25:38.550
We have a very dark sense
of humor in the teams.

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:42.260
And it became apparent rather quickly

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:45.280
that it was a bigger deal
than maybe I thought,

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:46.662
'cause I was mostly just annoyed

00:25:46.662 --> 00:25:49.628
that I had to deal with it at all.

00:25:49.628 --> 00:25:52.832
There was a lot going on that day.

00:25:52.832 --> 00:25:55.631
It was the Sunday before the election.

00:25:55.631 --> 00:25:57.850
We were doing pretty well,

00:25:57.850 --> 00:25:59.924
but I had a lot of events I had to get to

00:25:59.924 --> 00:26:02.520
and it felt more like an annoyance.

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:05.940
I was not used to having to
respond publicly to things,

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:07.237
probably the way I'm
more used to that now.

00:26:07.237 --> 00:26:09.878
- You wanna give people
the one-sentence recap

00:26:09.878 --> 00:26:11.546
of what the skit was,

00:26:11.546 --> 00:26:13.158
or what the line was there?

00:26:13.158 --> 00:26:14.530
- Oh, sure, sure.

00:26:14.530 --> 00:26:17.765
So, Pete Davidson was basically
going down a list of quote,

00:26:17.765 --> 00:26:20.220
unquote gross people, all politicians.

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:24.063
All, except for one, were Republican,

00:26:24.063 --> 00:26:27.460
and he basically just
makes fun of everybody.

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:29.287
But with me, at first, he's like,

00:26:29.287 --> 00:26:31.618
"This guy, he looks kinda
like a hitman in a porno."

00:26:31.618 --> 00:26:34.481
And that was pretty funny,
to be honest (laughs).

00:26:34.481 --> 00:26:36.347
But then he goes,

00:26:36.347 --> 00:26:38.167
"I'm sorry, I know he lost his eye

00:26:38.167 --> 00:26:40.100
"in war or whatever," right?

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:42.153
And it was that single line

00:26:42.153 --> 00:26:45.408
that seemed so dismissive of a war injury

00:26:45.408 --> 00:26:47.320
that just it drove people nuts,

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:49.253
and understandably so.

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:53.060
Now, the way I analyze this,

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:55.693
and the way I analyze it in my book,

00:26:56.752 --> 00:27:00.072
is it was pretty obvious

00:27:00.072 --> 00:27:03.430
that he was trying to take a
dig at conservatives, right?

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:05.985
It's Pete Davidson, it's
Saturday Night Live.

00:27:05.985 --> 00:27:07.970
They dig at Democrats plenty too,

00:27:07.970 --> 00:27:10.640
but on this particular skit,

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:13.580
it was a very clear
swipe at Conservatives.

00:27:13.580 --> 00:27:18.258
Because it's obvious, just watch it.

00:27:18.258 --> 00:27:21.650
It was not as obvious that he was trying

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:24.425
to insult wounded veterans.

00:27:24.425 --> 00:27:27.988
And so, the thought
process the next day was,

00:27:27.988 --> 00:27:30.309
okay, how much grace can we give here,

00:27:30.309 --> 00:27:33.120
while still acknowledging

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:35.110
that many veterans are hurt by this?

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:36.860
Genuinely, they're pissed off,

00:27:36.860 --> 00:27:38.300
and maybe they have good reason to be.

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:40.750
But also, I don't wanna
unleash this outrage mob.

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:42.505
Because one, I don't feel it.

00:27:42.505 --> 00:27:47.505
I just, I genuinely don't feel
this sense of being offended,

00:27:47.643 --> 00:27:48.909
and that's just not who I am.

00:27:48.909 --> 00:27:50.950
And so, I don't wanna express something

00:27:50.950 --> 00:27:53.820
that I'm not really feeling either,

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:55.216
which seems to be the norm.

00:27:55.216 --> 00:27:56.730
And the surprising thing

00:27:56.730 --> 00:28:00.560
about all this was that it
was so surprising to people

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:02.610
that I wouldn't unleash the outrage mob.

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:03.551
And I said something,

00:28:03.551 --> 00:28:07.378
my main quote from that was a quote

00:28:07.378 --> 00:28:09.368
that I actually stole
from a Harvard professor,

00:28:09.368 --> 00:28:12.267
which was "Try hard not to offend people,

00:28:12.267 --> 00:28:13.864
"try even harder not to be offended."

00:28:13.864 --> 00:28:15.633
The joke wasn't very funny,

00:28:15.633 --> 00:28:18.223
but I'm not gonna call
for this guy to get fired.

00:28:18.223 --> 00:28:19.986
I'm not gonna demand

00:28:19.986 --> 00:28:22.968
that everybody cancel Saturday Night Live.

00:28:22.968 --> 00:28:26.388
It just felt wrong, and so I didn't do it.

00:28:26.388 --> 00:28:29.422
And that gave Saturday
Night Live the space

00:28:29.422 --> 00:28:32.470
to actually apologize
the way they wanted to

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:33.303
and have me on.

00:28:33.303 --> 00:28:34.613
And I talk about this in my,

00:28:34.613 --> 00:28:37.970
I have a whole chapter about
the right sense of shame,

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:39.179
and this is the only time I bring

00:28:39.179 --> 00:28:40.968
up the Saturday Night
Live thing in the book.

00:28:40.968 --> 00:28:44.790
The book kinda came
about because of the fame

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:46.580
that I got from Saturday Night Live,

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:48.000
but the book isn't really about that.

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:51.348
It just so happens that
I was directly involved

00:28:51.348 --> 00:28:54.070
in sort of an outrage culture moment.

00:28:54.070 --> 00:28:56.670
And so, I decided to write a book

00:28:56.670 --> 00:28:58.642
about the solutions to outrage culture,

00:28:58.642 --> 00:29:03.642
but that moment takes up three
pages of the book, not even.

00:29:04.796 --> 00:29:06.580
But what I point out is

00:29:06.580 --> 00:29:09.870
that there's a right way to
show shame in our culture,

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:11.650
and there's a wrong way.

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:12.550
And more and more and more,

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:14.730
our culture is going the wrong way,

00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:16.507
where we have these two extreme options.

00:29:16.507 --> 00:29:20.520
On the one hand, people will
bow down to the outrage model,

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:22.350
I'm so sorry, I can't believe I did that.

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:25.740
And I bring up some examples
in the book on that.

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:30.001
One, because this one just
drove me nuts, was Mario Lopez.

00:29:30.001 --> 00:29:34.550
When he makes the all too
understandable comment

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:36.720
that three-year-olds should
not choose their gender.

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:39.373
I mean, oh my god, can you
even believe he said that?

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:41.360
- What a radical.

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:42.957
- He got slurge for it, right?

00:29:42.957 --> 00:29:44.637
But what does he do, he doesn't say,

00:29:44.637 --> 00:29:45.857
"Hey, hold on.

00:29:45.857 --> 00:29:47.117
"What's wrong with that?

00:29:47.117 --> 00:29:48.599
"Come on, guys."

00:29:48.599 --> 00:29:51.458
He doesn't do that. He says,
"Oh my god, I'm so sorry.

00:29:51.458 --> 00:29:52.778
"I'm so, so sorry.

00:29:52.778 --> 00:29:54.228
"Please forgive me," right?

00:29:54.228 --> 00:29:56.343
That's the wrong sense of shame.

00:29:57.506 --> 00:30:00.680
On the other extreme, there's
another wrong sense of shame,

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:02.325
which is showing no shame at all.

00:30:02.325 --> 00:30:04.359
And there's plenty of examples like that,

00:30:04.359 --> 00:30:06.333
mostly in the political world,

00:30:06.333 --> 00:30:10.386
but in other places as well
and other public figures.

00:30:10.386 --> 00:30:12.032
And then, I diagnose,

00:30:12.032 --> 00:30:13.559
why is that?

00:30:13.559 --> 00:30:16.492
Why is there this incentive
for one or the other?

00:30:16.492 --> 00:30:19.400
And it's an all-of-us problem.

00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:21.258
It's not just our terrible politicians

00:30:21.258 --> 00:30:22.705
or our silly celebrities,

00:30:22.705 --> 00:30:24.460
it's an all-of-us problem.

00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:27.855
We all engage in this
shaming in the wrong way.

00:30:27.855 --> 00:30:31.176
We either, we overreact to such an extent

00:30:31.176 --> 00:30:33.757
that we force people to bow
down when they shouldn't,

00:30:33.757 --> 00:30:35.369
or we overreact to such an extent

00:30:35.369 --> 00:30:38.039
that public figures realize
there's no incentive

00:30:38.039 --> 00:30:40.465
to apologize because there's no forgiving.

00:30:40.465 --> 00:30:42.509
There's no redemption,
there's no forgetting.

00:30:42.509 --> 00:30:45.854
And so, we have to get back
to sort of this middle ground.

00:30:45.854 --> 00:30:48.600
And people, and there's good
examples that I bring up

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:51.250
in the book of that
good middle ground too.

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:53.990
And we just have to do more of that.

00:30:53.990 --> 00:30:55.660
And be more nuanced in our thinking.

00:30:55.660 --> 00:30:59.277
Nuance is a huge part of
solving this problem (laughs).

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:02.140
- So, you go back on SNL,

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:04.490
and there's humor, and
there's nuance, and decency,

00:31:04.490 --> 00:31:05.323
and all of those things,

00:31:05.323 --> 00:31:06.850
and that kind of propels you.

00:31:06.850 --> 00:31:08.150
And one of the things

00:31:08.150 --> 00:31:09.910
that I think is sort of
interesting about you is

00:31:09.910 --> 00:31:11.822
it's like we've sort of got
these cartoon characters

00:31:11.822 --> 00:31:14.820
on the left with The Squad,

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:17.270
like AOC and Ilhan Omar and Tlaib,

00:31:17.270 --> 00:31:18.216
and a couple of others.

00:31:18.216 --> 00:31:21.210
And then, there's sort of you
on the other side of that,

00:31:21.210 --> 00:31:23.580
and when you take you and the eye patch,

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:26.034
you're kind of like Nick Fury.

00:31:26.034 --> 00:31:28.660
We're looking at this
through a Marvel lens.

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:30.035
- As we should, as we should.

00:31:30.035 --> 00:31:31.558
- As we should, right.

00:31:31.558 --> 00:31:34.597
But I mean, all the politicians now,

00:31:34.597 --> 00:31:36.680
and Trump and everybody,

00:31:36.680 --> 00:31:38.940
it's sort of like characters in a movie,

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:40.571
or in a video game, or
something like that.

00:31:40.571 --> 00:31:42.150
But I mention this,

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:44.399
because it seems like
there's this young crew

00:31:44.399 --> 00:31:45.897
from the lefties,

00:31:45.897 --> 00:31:48.970
and then there's sort of you
on the other side of that.

00:31:48.970 --> 00:31:52.040
Are there some other
guys, girls, whatever,

00:31:52.040 --> 00:31:54.030
that you think are in your crew

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.610
that are sort of the younger,

00:31:55.610 --> 00:31:58.130
more get it, more willing to open up,

00:31:58.130 --> 00:31:59.217
talk about these things.

00:31:59.217 --> 00:32:00.689
I've done events with you

00:32:00.689 --> 00:32:03.323
where you can be out
there with the students,

00:32:03.323 --> 00:32:05.612
and it's not this stuffy thing.

00:32:05.612 --> 00:32:08.097
But its seems like it's
kinda just you holding that

00:32:08.097 --> 00:32:12.260
for the sorta new, more, maybe,
open-minded Conservatives,

00:32:12.260 --> 00:32:13.260
something like that.

00:32:14.449 --> 00:32:15.880
- That's a good question,

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:18.655
I do get asked that a lot and
it's a difficult question,

00:32:18.655 --> 00:32:21.370
because I don't wanna try
and analyze the mind's

00:32:21.370 --> 00:32:23.740
of my colleagues who
maybe don't put themselves

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:24.573
out their as much.

00:32:24.573 --> 00:32:26.430
I think maybe a lot would be willing to,

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:28.564
but they don't have the same ability to,

00:32:28.564 --> 00:32:29.785
or they're not sure how.

00:32:29.785 --> 00:32:32.690
There's some really
great members of Congress

00:32:32.690 --> 00:32:34.393
who are our age,

00:32:35.478 --> 00:32:37.352
like Mike Gallagher, Elise Stefanik,

00:32:37.352 --> 00:32:39.060
and they're out their,

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:42.040
but they have their own style.

00:32:42.040 --> 00:32:43.710
They're not on social media the way I am.

00:32:43.710 --> 00:32:45.141
They're not out there the way I am.

00:32:45.141 --> 00:32:48.017
Everybody does have their own style.

00:32:48.017 --> 00:32:51.801
I'm excited about sort of a
new squad coming out of Texas.

00:32:51.801 --> 00:32:55.222
We've got a lot of great
new candidates running.

00:32:55.222 --> 00:32:58.500
A very diverse set, very young.

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:00.625
I'm really excited about it.

00:33:00.625 --> 00:33:03.690
I've got Wesley Hunt here next to me,

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:06.500
an African-American army helicopter pilot,

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:08.899
got Tony Gonzales running
for Will Hurd's seat.

00:33:08.899 --> 00:33:11.714
Old Master Chief, not old, sorry.

00:33:11.714 --> 00:33:13.307
Prior Master Chief.

00:33:13.307 --> 00:33:16.248
Beth Van Duyne, she's a prior mayor.

00:33:16.248 --> 00:33:20.843
Again, young, very intelligent,
got a lot of spunk.

00:33:20.843 --> 00:33:24.120
Genevieve Collins, another great one.

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:27.042
Again, young woman, a millennial, like me.

00:33:27.042 --> 00:33:30.960
Very successful, got a
great shot at that seat.

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:33.401
There's a lot of really good candidates,

00:33:33.401 --> 00:33:35.785
and I think the Republican
Party does have a chance

00:33:35.785 --> 00:33:40.235
to really remake itself
in a really big way

00:33:40.235 --> 00:33:42.650
that connects with a
lot of different people.

00:33:42.650 --> 00:33:44.625
So, I'm excited about it.

00:33:44.625 --> 00:33:47.080
- Is the biggest there just getting

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:49.540
over sort of the old stereotypes?

00:33:49.540 --> 00:33:50.780
I mean, just the old stereotypes.

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:53.258
Conservatives care about
money and care about war.

00:33:53.258 --> 00:33:57.394
Democrats like poor people and peace.

00:33:57.394 --> 00:34:00.436
These basic, sort of very
factory setting things.

00:34:00.436 --> 00:34:01.603
When, in reality,

00:34:01.603 --> 00:34:05.120
I mean, we spoke at the Turning
Point Student Action Summit,

00:34:05.120 --> 00:34:06.830
and Trump spoke that morning,

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:09.726
and it was right after
the impeachment hearing,

00:34:09.726 --> 00:34:12.320
and you wanted to get up in
the middle of his speech.

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:14.070
You were trying to show him your vote,

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:15.057
that you voted against it.

00:34:15.057 --> 00:34:17.730
And it was a totally impromptu moment.

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:18.856
Trump looks at you in the crowd.

00:34:18.856 --> 00:34:20.625
He's like, "Dan, come on up here."

00:34:20.625 --> 00:34:22.687
The secret service guys
were all looking like,

00:34:22.687 --> 00:34:25.347
"Oh god, now we have to get
this guy from the crowd,

00:34:25.347 --> 00:34:26.180
"even though he's a congressman,

00:34:26.180 --> 00:34:27.650
"get him on stage."

00:34:27.650 --> 00:34:31.499
But my broader point is that
it was like fun and real,

00:34:31.499 --> 00:34:32.988
which is so the reverse

00:34:32.988 --> 00:34:36.406
of what politics is presented to us as.

00:34:36.406 --> 00:34:40.378
- I mean, it's totally
changed in the last few years.

00:34:40.378 --> 00:34:42.106
And I think it's exciting.

00:34:42.106 --> 00:34:43.621
Now, we have to be responsible

00:34:43.621 --> 00:34:46.987
with how we manage this new train

00:34:46.987 --> 00:34:49.759
that's just lurching forward, right.

00:34:49.759 --> 00:34:53.961
Because it is this sort
of interesting combination

00:34:53.961 --> 00:34:58.961
of showmanship with some deep thinking

00:34:59.398 --> 00:35:03.223
and some real, actual statesmanship.

00:35:04.216 --> 00:35:07.210
It is up to us as leaders to make sure

00:35:07.210 --> 00:35:09.732
that we maintain those bonds

00:35:09.732 --> 00:35:13.140
and that balance in a very coherent way.

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:15.310
And that's what I try to do.

00:35:15.310 --> 00:35:19.600
That's why I do more than
just the showy stuff.

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:22.977
We gotta have fun, and we gotta
show that we can have fun,

00:35:22.977 --> 00:35:23.960
but we also have to show

00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:27.383
that deeply and not fall into
that very simplistic mindset

00:35:27.383 --> 00:35:29.136
that you're one or the other.

00:35:29.136 --> 00:35:30.907
These guys are for poor people

00:35:30.907 --> 00:35:32.490
and these guys are for rich people, right.

00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:33.560
And you're totally right,

00:35:33.560 --> 00:35:35.240
there are these stereotypes

00:35:35.240 --> 00:35:37.640
that we have to resist and move past.

00:35:37.640 --> 00:35:39.482
And for too long the Republican
Party has been unable

00:35:39.482 --> 00:35:40.608
to do that,

00:35:40.608 --> 00:35:43.040
because we didn't quite take the time

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:45.209
to understand the left, I think.

00:35:45.209 --> 00:35:47.095
And I think, what I'm excited about,

00:35:47.095 --> 00:35:48.460
the younger generation

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:50.531
of Conservatives takes a lot more time

00:35:50.531 --> 00:35:54.760
to try and understand the
left, and how they think.

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:58.185
Maybe, even what they're right about.

00:35:58.185 --> 00:36:01.170
But how to confront their
policy prescriptions

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:05.967
to those problems that they try to expose.

00:36:05.967 --> 00:36:09.210
And thinking more deeply
about our foundations,

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:10.810
about why we think what we think.

00:36:10.810 --> 00:36:14.552
Young people, especially,
because they're so well-informed.

00:36:14.552 --> 00:36:17.530
And that's a careful word to use,

00:36:17.530 --> 00:36:18.551
because they're informed,

00:36:18.551 --> 00:36:19.820
but they don't have experience

00:36:19.820 --> 00:36:22.673
with which to process a
lot of that information.

00:36:22.673 --> 00:36:23.970
But generally, what you see

00:36:23.970 --> 00:36:25.950
with young people is they
want more information,

00:36:25.950 --> 00:36:27.328
they want to listen to
an hour long podcast.

00:36:27.328 --> 00:36:31.971
They want a deeper explanation,
not just a talking point,

00:36:31.971 --> 00:36:33.910
of why you're saying what you're saying,

00:36:33.910 --> 00:36:35.680
which is why I do these,
"Here's the Truth" videos.

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:38.530
And I still have to
make them really short,

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:39.678
because it's social media.

00:36:39.678 --> 00:36:43.125
I see the percentages people watch.

00:36:43.125 --> 00:36:43.958
It's still short.

00:36:43.958 --> 00:36:44.860
- Small steps.

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:47.690
- And I also do a podcast
for the same reason,

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:50.493
so that I can access
people that way as well.

00:36:50.493 --> 00:36:52.417
I mean, you're gotta meet
people where they're at.

00:36:52.417 --> 00:36:54.612
And so, that is the
new future of politics.

00:36:54.612 --> 00:36:57.141
It's not just getting that news hit

00:36:57.141 --> 00:36:58.404
or getting that op-ed written.

00:36:58.404 --> 00:37:00.790
Because, again, only certain
people read that stuff.

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:02.171
Only certain people watch that stuff.

00:37:02.171 --> 00:37:05.060
Everybody else is over
on YouTube and Instagram.

00:37:05.060 --> 00:37:06.190
Then, you've got your Facebook crowd.

00:37:06.190 --> 00:37:10.330
And you just gotta be everywhere,
if you wanna be effective.

00:37:10.330 --> 00:37:12.540
- So, I love the inner flap of the book.

00:37:12.540 --> 00:37:15.136
It says, "Jordan Peterson's
'12 Rules for Life'

00:37:15.136 --> 00:37:19.497
"meets Jocko Willink and Lief
Babin's 'Extreme Ownership'

00:37:19.497 --> 00:37:21.557
"in this tough-love leadership book

00:37:21.557 --> 00:37:25.465
"from a Navy SEAL and rising
star in Republican politics."

00:37:25.465 --> 00:37:27.990
So, obviously, you know
I know Jordan quite well.

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:30.319
And, of course, I've had Jocko on as well.

00:37:30.319 --> 00:37:33.120
Was that sort of the idea there?

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:35.120
To fuse together these people

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:38.770
that were sort of about putting
your life together first

00:37:38.770 --> 00:37:41.683
and then also blending your experience,

00:37:43.565 --> 00:37:44.693
being a SEAL, now being in politics,

00:37:46.569 --> 00:37:49.490
that it's about sort of
fixing yourself first,

00:37:49.490 --> 00:37:52.523
which is very consistently
a Jordan-type message?

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:58.290
- It is, and I think the fact

00:37:59.696 --> 00:38:02.745
that it did become this combination

00:38:02.745 --> 00:38:06.440
of Jordan's book with
Lief and Jocko's book,

00:38:06.440 --> 00:38:10.373
that was a bit more of an
after-the-fact thought.

00:38:12.430 --> 00:38:14.260
But that was the point, for sure.

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:17.060
And I think that's a very
accurate description of it.

00:38:17.060 --> 00:38:19.270
I'm not quite sure that I would
call it a leadership book.

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:23.170
I might edit that little part
out on the next set of copies.

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:24.297
- Next round, next round.

00:38:24.297 --> 00:38:27.720
- 'Cause it's fundamentally
a mental resilience book

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:29.560
and how to be a stronger person,

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:31.010
and therefore a stronger culture.

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:33.379
And so, it's meant to be both.

00:38:33.379 --> 00:38:36.520
It's meant to be a personal journey,

00:38:36.520 --> 00:38:38.530
but, as well as, a cultural journey.

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:41.056
I am fighting the culture
war in this book as well.

00:38:41.056 --> 00:38:43.709
This culture war against
victimhood ideology.

00:38:43.709 --> 00:38:46.811
That's a prevalent theme
throughout the book.

00:38:46.811 --> 00:38:51.012
But I'm also bringing it
down to very practical terms

00:38:51.012 --> 00:38:53.418
to your daily life.

00:38:53.418 --> 00:38:55.709
But I'm also doing so with,

00:38:55.709 --> 00:38:59.427
not quite the extensive,
philosophical underpinnings

00:38:59.427 --> 00:39:02.494
that Jordan Peterson does,
but I'm still doing that.

00:39:02.494 --> 00:39:05.780
I'm still diving into
psychological research

00:39:05.780 --> 00:39:08.450
and all the literature to
back up what I'm saying.

00:39:08.450 --> 00:39:10.713
'Cause one thing I point out
in the book quite often is

00:39:10.713 --> 00:39:12.207
that these are not new ideas

00:39:12.207 --> 00:39:15.010
, but that's the kinda the point.

00:39:15.010 --> 00:39:16.730
The best ideas are the old ideas.

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:18.660
They've been around the
longest for a very good reason.

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:19.820
They've been through trail and error.

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:22.519
They've been passed down for
generations because they work.

00:39:22.519 --> 00:39:24.930
Personal responsibility
is the tenant of our,

00:39:24.930 --> 00:39:27.735
is a cultural foundation because it works.

00:39:27.735 --> 00:39:30.100
Because it means all of these things

00:39:30.100 --> 00:39:32.826
that are very important
for a society to thrive.

00:39:32.826 --> 00:39:36.530
Thinking through your
emotions before you react,

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:39.870
that's an old piece of advice.

00:39:39.870 --> 00:39:40.703
Like your mom said,

00:39:40.703 --> 00:39:42.900
"Count to 10 before you
start to say something."

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:46.850
There's a reason that
these sort of idioms exist

00:39:46.850 --> 00:39:49.700
in our culture, because
they're old and they work.

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:52.610
I referenced the Bible
quite a bit in the book,

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:55.402
because wow, these ideas, they're not new.

00:39:55.402 --> 00:39:56.430
They're not new.

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:58.090
And then they're backed up by science,

00:39:58.090 --> 00:39:59.559
they're backed up by our intuition,

00:39:59.559 --> 00:40:02.163
they're backed up by personal experience.

00:40:04.521 --> 00:40:06.930
'Cause my mind goes a lot
of different directions,

00:40:06.930 --> 00:40:08.610
and so I wanted to write this sort

00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:09.961
of multi-disciplinary book

00:40:09.961 --> 00:40:12.884
and then try to draw
those disciplines together

00:40:12.884 --> 00:40:14.462
in a single theme

00:40:14.462 --> 00:40:18.266
that is simultaneously very entertaining

00:40:18.266 --> 00:40:22.360
and also speaks to people
in a modern way too.

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:24.790
Because I've referenced pop culture a lot.

00:40:24.790 --> 00:40:25.923
I've referenced movies a lot.

00:40:25.923 --> 00:40:27.670
I've referenced people that you would know

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:28.618
in politics a lot.

00:40:28.618 --> 00:40:32.230
Not until the end of
the book do I really get

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:35.772
into, I think, a political
philosophy discussion.

00:40:35.772 --> 00:40:39.222
- Dan, are you saying
that people before you

00:40:39.222 --> 00:40:42.158
and before me actually had good ideas,

00:40:42.158 --> 00:40:45.472
and we should possibly
listen to maybe the founders,

00:40:45.472 --> 00:40:49.620
and people even before that
that maybe helped us get here,

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:51.788
and it shouldn't just be whatever
we think on any given day

00:40:51.788 --> 00:40:53.963
and that our grandparents
weren't all idiots?

00:40:53.963 --> 00:40:55.160
Are you saying that?

00:40:55.160 --> 00:40:57.479
- I might be saying that, yeah.

00:40:57.479 --> 00:41:02.287
To a large extent,
conservatism means gratitude.

00:41:02.287 --> 00:41:05.470
It's gratitude for things that work,

00:41:05.470 --> 00:41:07.058
like philosophies that work,

00:41:07.058 --> 00:41:09.080
cultural foundations that work,

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:10.502
policies that work.

00:41:10.502 --> 00:41:12.903
For meaning to conserve anything,

00:41:12.903 --> 00:41:14.564
it's conserving the things that work.

00:41:14.564 --> 00:41:17.430
And what the left fundamentally stands

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:19.003
for is radical change.

00:41:19.003 --> 00:41:21.746
This sort of reaching for utopia,

00:41:21.746 --> 00:41:24.189
this constant reach for utopia.

00:41:24.189 --> 00:41:29.189
And a constant tearing
down of our current status

00:41:29.823 --> 00:41:33.323
in order to justify the
really extreme measures

00:41:33.323 --> 00:41:35.596
that they need to take in order to get

00:41:35.596 --> 00:41:37.544
to this supposed utopia.

00:41:37.544 --> 00:41:38.906
And that's dangerous.

00:41:38.906 --> 00:41:41.422
It's dangerous because
it leaves out so much

00:41:41.422 --> 00:41:42.504
of the equation,

00:41:42.504 --> 00:41:45.131
like how did you almost get to the utopia

00:41:45.131 --> 00:41:46.430
in the first place?

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:47.950
'Cause you have to admit
that we have it better

00:41:47.950 --> 00:41:51.225
than anybody ever in history.

00:41:51.225 --> 00:41:53.084
That doesn't mean things are perfect,

00:41:53.084 --> 00:41:55.406
but that is the nature of our being.

00:41:55.406 --> 00:41:57.452
And what does perfect look like?

00:41:57.452 --> 00:42:00.440
So, these are deep questions,

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:02.200
but they treat them as if they're not.

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:03.943
That's the problem with Progressives.

00:42:03.943 --> 00:42:06.410
These are very fundamental questions,

00:42:06.410 --> 00:42:07.859
but they treat them very casually.

00:42:07.859 --> 00:42:10.497
And Conservatives are the ones that say,

00:42:10.497 --> 00:42:11.597
"Hold on, guys.

00:42:11.597 --> 00:42:13.357
"Think, think through this.

00:42:13.357 --> 00:42:15.217
"Think of the second, third-order effects

00:42:15.217 --> 00:42:17.667
"of what you're saying when you want

00:42:17.667 --> 00:42:18.757
"to implement something simple

00:42:18.757 --> 00:42:20.577
"like a $15 minimum wage."

00:42:20.577 --> 00:42:21.540
Really?

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:24.806
There's deeper consequences
to every action you take,

00:42:24.806 --> 00:42:28.720
and Conservatives I think fight
to recognize those things.

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:32.500
- So, as a guy that has
fought in actual wars,

00:42:32.500 --> 00:42:34.990
and then you referenced
the cultural war a couple

00:42:34.990 --> 00:42:35.823
of times here.

00:42:35.823 --> 00:42:37.620
What are some of the
lessons you learned just

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:40.565
through discipline, and
training, and everything else.

00:42:40.565 --> 00:42:42.820
Through actual, physical war

00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:45.618
versus the sort of war
that you're fighting

00:42:45.618 --> 00:42:48.143
on the ideological front now?

00:42:50.167 --> 00:42:51.910
- Well, every job I've had,

00:42:51.910 --> 00:42:53.740
there's somebody who threatens to kill me,

00:42:53.740 --> 00:42:56.147
so it hasn't changed
all that much (laughs).

00:42:57.123 --> 00:43:00.040
Dark humor (laughs)

00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:02.157
- It's Twitter man, it's rough (laughs).

00:43:02.157 --> 00:43:07.157
- No, it's just real war,
so to speak, is very honest.

00:43:10.284 --> 00:43:12.270
Your decisions, your attention to detail,

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:14.280
they have these
life-or-death consequences.

00:43:14.280 --> 00:43:18.755
The culture war, it's less honest.

00:43:18.755 --> 00:43:21.940
It's about opportunism oftentimes.

00:43:21.940 --> 00:43:24.868
The political warfare is
about opportunism for sure.

00:43:24.868 --> 00:43:28.635
It's about trying to interpret
the worst of intentions

00:43:28.635 --> 00:43:31.126
based off of your opponents actions,

00:43:31.126 --> 00:43:33.513
even if that's not what they meant.

00:43:35.677 --> 00:43:38.078
It's a blood sport none of itself,

00:43:38.078 --> 00:43:39.903
it's just not literal blood.

00:43:43.703 --> 00:43:45.860
And that was the premise for a lot

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:47.490
of me wanting to write this book,

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:49.396
because I see how damaging that is.

00:43:49.396 --> 00:43:50.970
It's causing anxiety

00:43:50.970 --> 00:43:52.440
on a micro level.

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:54.950
It's causing people to
see the doctor more.

00:43:54.950 --> 00:43:56.874
It's making people upset.

00:43:56.874 --> 00:43:58.068
You see these happy people,

00:43:58.068 --> 00:43:59.437
but how could a happy person write

00:43:59.437 --> 00:44:01.000
what you just wrote on Twitter?

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:02.860
That's a terrible thing to say.

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:04.690
What is going on here?

00:44:04.690 --> 00:44:08.320
And why are students so upset?

00:44:08.320 --> 00:44:11.140
Why do they feel like their
physical safety is in jeopardy

00:44:11.140 --> 00:44:13.749
just because somebody's speaking an idea

00:44:13.749 --> 00:44:15.050
that they don't like?

00:44:15.050 --> 00:44:17.521
That's a really strange thing.

00:44:17.521 --> 00:44:22.170
And I don't diagnose that problem too much

00:44:22.170 --> 00:44:23.003
in my book.

00:44:23.003 --> 00:44:24.388
I leave that to better authors,

00:44:24.388 --> 00:44:26.015
and I just reference their work.

00:44:26.015 --> 00:44:27.249
Because fundamentally,

00:44:27.249 --> 00:44:31.700
I'm trying to find the solutions to that.

00:44:31.700 --> 00:44:32.760
One, I'm hoping

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:35.129
that the people I'm describing
would actually read the book,

00:44:35.129 --> 00:44:37.983
instead of the people who
are, generally, probably,

00:44:37.983 --> 00:44:40.763
don't need the book (laughs)
are the one's buying it.

00:44:42.360 --> 00:44:44.300
But I'm hoping that one
thing will speak to you

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:47.660
and just cause you to sort
of shift your thinking

00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:50.197
on something and maybe
see some of that problem

00:44:50.197 --> 00:44:52.161
in yourself when you
didn't even realize it

00:44:52.161 --> 00:44:54.721
and change accordingly.

00:44:54.721 --> 00:44:57.021
I know, even writing the book,

00:44:57.021 --> 00:44:59.382
I probably figured some
things out about myself.

00:44:59.382 --> 00:45:03.471
This book is not about
how perfect I am (laughs).

00:45:03.471 --> 00:45:05.293
I just happen to write about the standards

00:45:05.293 --> 00:45:07.053
by which I think I should live.

00:45:07.939 --> 00:45:10.300
- As someone that just
finished my first book too,

00:45:10.300 --> 00:45:11.180
I hear you.

00:45:11.180 --> 00:45:13.260
It actually, it unearths stuff within you,

00:45:13.260 --> 00:45:15.083
and that's actually the cool part.

00:45:16.816 --> 00:45:19.690
So, I know we could agree
on most of our frustrations

00:45:19.690 --> 00:45:20.730
with the left all day long,

00:45:20.730 --> 00:45:21.750
and we could talk about it

00:45:21.750 --> 00:45:24.300
for 20 hours in a row, no problem.

00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:26.595
What would you say are
some of the frustrations

00:45:26.595 --> 00:45:29.000
that you have with, say, your side,

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:31.546
or the Conservative movement,
or the Republican Party?

00:45:31.546 --> 00:45:34.010
And when you talk to somebody like me,

00:45:34.010 --> 00:45:36.048
who you know we have some
political disagreements,

00:45:36.048 --> 00:45:37.730
do you think there is,

00:45:37.730 --> 00:45:41.925
how wide do you think that
tent on the right can get,

00:45:41.925 --> 00:45:44.510
without losing those core principles?

00:45:44.510 --> 00:45:46.364
I've had people like Heather McDonald

00:45:46.364 --> 00:45:48.597
on the show from the Manhattan Institute,

00:45:48.597 --> 00:45:50.890
who's a totally secular Conservative,

00:45:50.890 --> 00:45:52.239
she's an atheist Conservative.

00:45:52.239 --> 00:45:54.150
But a lot of Conservatives think,

00:45:54.150 --> 00:45:56.701
oh, no, there has to be
a religious attachment.

00:45:56.701 --> 00:45:58.583
Or, is there even any room for,

00:45:58.583 --> 00:46:01.781
I describe myself as
begrudgingly pro-choice,

00:46:01.781 --> 00:46:04.660
and I get into it in
the book pretty heavily.

00:46:04.660 --> 00:46:09.013
How do you figure out what
room there is on this side

00:46:09.013 --> 00:46:12.530
for people that have sort
of marginal differences,

00:46:12.530 --> 00:46:14.310
let's say, but mostly believe

00:46:14.310 --> 00:46:16.079
in individual rights, liberty, freedom,

00:46:16.079 --> 00:46:18.741
all the stuff that really
is the important stuff?

00:46:18.741 --> 00:46:23.310
- That's an internally debated question.

00:46:23.310 --> 00:46:25.520
I don't know that I have
a great answer for it.

00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:29.030
It largely depends I think
on your own temperament

00:46:29.971 --> 00:46:34.835
and my personal temperament is
I'm certainly more accepting

00:46:34.835 --> 00:46:38.264
of people who disagree
with 10% of my views.

00:46:38.264 --> 00:46:42.007
And say that, "Fine, that
still makes you a Republican.

00:46:42.007 --> 00:46:43.206
"I'm not gonna yell and scream at you,

00:46:43.206 --> 00:46:46.731
"because we disagree on a couple
of things here and there."

00:46:46.731 --> 00:46:50.025
I think it's possible to
wrap conservative ideology

00:46:50.025 --> 00:46:55.025
into most of our views,

00:46:55.234 --> 00:46:59.853
and it takes, I think,
a sophisticated thinker

00:46:59.853 --> 00:47:01.308
to go through that.

00:47:01.308 --> 00:47:04.990
When you ask my frustrations
with Conservatives,

00:47:04.990 --> 00:47:07.350
it's not even, what I point
out in the book a lot,

00:47:07.350 --> 00:47:10.000
'cause I try to be fair,
as fair as possible,

00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:12.094
as fair as I can in the book,

00:47:12.094 --> 00:47:15.085
when I'm criticizing Liberals,

00:47:15.085 --> 00:47:18.180
and I also criticize Conservatives.

00:47:18.180 --> 00:47:21.110
If I have a Liberal politician example

00:47:21.110 --> 00:47:22.264
of something bad that happened,

00:47:22.264 --> 00:47:24.410
try to have a Conservative one as well,

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:25.655
to point out the same.

00:47:25.655 --> 00:47:27.885
It pertains to outrage culture,

00:47:27.885 --> 00:47:32.832
specifically with this tendency

00:47:32.832 --> 00:47:37.832
to elevate passion and
emotion above sophistication,

00:47:38.834 --> 00:47:43.834
replacing sophisticated
arguments with attitude.

00:47:43.892 --> 00:47:45.284
I have a problem with that

00:47:45.284 --> 00:47:48.519
and Conservatives certainly do it.

00:47:48.519 --> 00:47:52.040
- So, where does Trump fall
into that for you then,

00:47:52.040 --> 00:47:54.210
because I know you are a,

00:47:54.210 --> 00:47:55.890
I always hate the phrase Trump supporter.

00:47:55.890 --> 00:47:58.020
It's like, people, you
wanna support the president.

00:47:58.020 --> 00:48:00.630
He';s flying the plane, we
don't want this plane to crash.

00:48:00.630 --> 00:48:02.820
But, I mean, as a guy that
basically, you like Trump.

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:04.941
You support his agenda pretty
much from what I an tell.

00:48:04.941 --> 00:48:08.757
Where does he factor into
exactly what you just said there?

00:48:08.757 --> 00:48:11.533
- Not exactly with what I just said.

00:48:11.533 --> 00:48:14.520
I mean, he's not perfect (laughs), right?

00:48:14.520 --> 00:48:17.090
His style gets in his own way, for sure.

00:48:17.090 --> 00:48:19.150
And it's not my style.

00:48:19.150 --> 00:48:23.130
But as far as specifically
tyng it to what I just said,

00:48:23.130 --> 00:48:26.750
that's more prevalent
among activists to try

00:48:26.750 --> 00:48:30.826
and own the left in this
kinda disingenuous way.

00:48:30.826 --> 00:48:33.460
And sometimes it's not disingenuous,

00:48:33.460 --> 00:48:35.056
sometimes it's a great own, okay.

00:48:35.056 --> 00:48:37.063
But we just have to be
smart enough to distinguish

00:48:37.063 --> 00:48:38.207
between the two,

00:48:38.207 --> 00:48:41.285
and sometimes I see us not
distinguishing correctly.

00:48:41.285 --> 00:48:45.700
If I can immediately say
what the left would say

00:48:45.700 --> 00:48:46.960
to that argument,

00:48:46.960 --> 00:48:49.643
then you haven't thought
through your argument very well.

00:48:50.624 --> 00:48:52.810
This is why I like Ben Shapiro so much.

00:48:52.810 --> 00:48:55.439
Because he's very airtight
with his argumentation.

00:48:55.439 --> 00:48:58.820
You have to anticipate what
the other side is gonna say.

00:48:58.820 --> 00:49:01.450
That's what makes you good, right.

00:49:01.450 --> 00:49:02.803
Because you're right.

00:49:02.803 --> 00:49:07.122
'Cause you're actually
directly confronting

00:49:07.122 --> 00:49:09.590
what the other side is saying,

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:11.317
as opposed to building
a straw man argument.

00:49:11.317 --> 00:49:14.183
And when I say elevate
passion above substance,

00:49:14.183 --> 00:49:15.657
generally speaking,

00:49:15.657 --> 00:49:18.783
it means kinda making a
disingenuous straw man argument,

00:49:18.783 --> 00:49:21.005
and maybe I've done it.

00:49:21.005 --> 00:49:26.005
I'm sure I have. But we should avoid it.

00:49:26.670 --> 00:49:31.590
And what I also talk about
in my book is I'm not mad

00:49:31.590 --> 00:49:33.760
that that happens, I guess,
what frustrates me is that

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:35.983
that's what gets the most clicks.

00:49:38.260 --> 00:49:39.357
And I talk about this in a chapter,

00:49:39.357 --> 00:49:42.130
it's a really deep discussion, I think.

00:49:42.130 --> 00:49:43.400
I don't know if Jordan
Peterson would agree,

00:49:43.400 --> 00:49:44.770
'cause he's like how can you be deeper

00:49:44.770 --> 00:49:45.899
than Jordan Peterson (laughs)?

00:49:45.899 --> 00:49:48.840
But it's actually about
something that he talks

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:50.303
about quite a bit which is hero archetypes

00:49:50.303 --> 00:49:52.322
and what we view as good.

00:49:52.322 --> 00:49:55.090
And that's a good place to start.

00:49:55.090 --> 00:49:57.010
If we're trying to be better,

00:49:57.010 --> 00:49:59.835
well, we have to define what
better actually looks like

00:49:59.835 --> 00:50:01.069
and what works.

00:50:01.069 --> 00:50:04.010
And so, what we've done in
our culture is we've sort

00:50:04.010 --> 00:50:05.876
of elevated the wrong heroes over time,

00:50:05.876 --> 00:50:08.500
like we've elevated
victims instead of victors,

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:12.130
and I use Superman as
an example of this sort

00:50:12.130 --> 00:50:14.274
of heroic archetype that
we should look up to.

00:50:14.274 --> 00:50:17.519
Jesus is a hero that we look up to.

00:50:17.519 --> 00:50:20.123
It's like everything he
did was just perfect.

00:50:21.489 --> 00:50:24.149
But these days, these
days, Superman would have

00:50:24.149 --> 00:50:25.946
to be a victim, right?

00:50:25.946 --> 00:50:28.349
If we were to actually
look at him as a hero,

00:50:28.349 --> 00:50:31.270
then he would have to have
been victimized in some way.

00:50:31.270 --> 00:50:33.420
So, there's a real change there.

00:50:33.420 --> 00:50:35.140
And there's a change
in what we view as good

00:50:35.140 --> 00:50:38.483
and as it pertains to that
sort of extra attitude,

00:50:39.950 --> 00:50:42.881
passionate, own-the-libs type thinking.

00:50:42.881 --> 00:50:45.868
Well, shouldn't we be
elevating the smartest person

00:50:45.868 --> 00:50:47.350
in the room?

00:50:47.350 --> 00:50:50.071
Shouldn't we be elevating
that think tank piece

00:50:50.071 --> 00:50:53.700
that just in detail debunks
what the other side thinks

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:54.910
on a given policy?

00:50:54.910 --> 00:50:56.650
But that doesn't give
any clicks, and I get it.

00:50:56.650 --> 00:50:58.311
It's not emotional, it's not fun.

00:50:58.311 --> 00:50:59.743
It's not entertaining.

00:50:59.743 --> 00:51:01.429
But maybe, we should rethink that.

00:51:01.429 --> 00:51:03.856
Maybe we should be
giving these think tanks

00:51:03.856 --> 00:51:06.000
a little bit more juice,

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:10.990
instead of our activists

00:51:10.990 --> 00:51:12.818
who have the snarkiest comment.

00:51:12.818 --> 00:51:16.660
And, again, I'm not
dispelling the importance

00:51:16.660 --> 00:51:18.820
and the fun behind a lot of the memes

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:20.550
that we like, make and share.

00:51:20.550 --> 00:51:21.446
I share them too.

00:51:21.446 --> 00:51:25.049
I just want us to think twice
and just to balance it out.

00:51:25.049 --> 00:51:27.940
- It's funny because when
I've been asked something like

00:51:27.940 --> 00:51:30.550
that I think of it in a war perspective.

00:51:30.550 --> 00:51:32.540
It's like the memes and some of that

00:51:32.540 --> 00:51:34.788
that's like the ground troops,
the ideas is the Air Force.

00:51:34.788 --> 00:51:38.909
You do need to hit this
from a multi-prong approach.

00:51:38.909 --> 00:51:41.470
So, it all sort of is part of it.

00:51:41.470 --> 00:51:43.740
So, we've talked this far about outrage,

00:51:43.740 --> 00:51:45.120
and culture war, and everything else.

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:48.050
And we haven't really
mentioned the media at all.

00:51:48.050 --> 00:51:49.570
Now, you know my feelings

00:51:49.570 --> 00:51:50.890
about most of the mainstream media.

00:51:50.890 --> 00:51:53.750
I think you share many of my opinions

00:51:54.710 --> 00:51:56.859
right now as it stands.

00:51:56.859 --> 00:52:01.070
What do you make of what is
left of the mainstream media,

00:52:01.070 --> 00:52:02.881
does it even matter anymore?

00:52:02.881 --> 00:52:05.665
Partly, I think it's just
sort of the show must go on.

00:52:05.665 --> 00:52:09.061
It's the last thing that keeps
us all cohesive as a nation.

00:52:09.061 --> 00:52:11.660
To have something
thought of as mainstream,

00:52:11.660 --> 00:52:14.390
otherwise we would all
completely splinter off

00:52:14.390 --> 00:52:17.750
into our own echo chambers,
and into our own ideologies,

00:52:17.750 --> 00:52:19.358
and all that.

00:52:19.358 --> 00:52:21.510
Is there anything that can be redeemed

00:52:21.510 --> 00:52:23.340
when you see what the
New York Times is up to,

00:52:23.340 --> 00:52:24.380
what CNN is up to?

00:52:24.380 --> 00:52:28.605
Is it worth redeeming, or do
we just need new institutions?

00:52:28.605 --> 00:52:31.740
- I had Dana Perino on
my podcast last week,

00:52:31.740 --> 00:52:33.529
and I was really pressing
her on this question

00:52:33.529 --> 00:52:35.789
about the behavior, the media,

00:52:35.789 --> 00:52:37.713
and she was really
pushing back against me,

00:52:37.713 --> 00:52:39.409
which I wasn't quite expecting,

00:52:39.409 --> 00:52:43.970
And she felt obliged to
rightfully point out to me

00:52:43.970 --> 00:52:47.240
that there's a lot of other media, right.

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:51.020
And it's not just the CNN
and MSNBC's which are truly

00:52:51.020 --> 00:52:54.423
in an information warfare
against the president.

00:52:54.423 --> 00:52:57.310
That's what they see their mission as,

00:52:57.310 --> 00:52:59.580
The New York Times similarly.

00:52:59.580 --> 00:53:01.814
Washington Post is somewhat more balanced,

00:53:01.814 --> 00:53:04.335
but overall the same thing.

00:53:04.335 --> 00:53:05.770
But what she had to point out

00:53:05.770 --> 00:53:10.770
to me was there's all these
other shows, or networks,

00:53:11.670 --> 00:53:12.820
and what's the right?

00:53:12.820 --> 00:53:14.730
I'm gonna screw up the exact terminology,

00:53:14.730 --> 00:53:17.490
but it's the difference
between cable and broadcast.

00:53:17.490 --> 00:53:18.490
I'm not really sure.

00:53:19.850 --> 00:53:22.406
Like ABC, and NBC, and CBS.

00:53:22.406 --> 00:53:27.406
And she sent me this Wall
Street Journal article,

00:53:27.406 --> 00:53:29.720
I think that came out today.

00:53:29.720 --> 00:53:31.167
And the Wall Street Journal article says,

00:53:31.167 --> 00:53:32.697
"Wow, look what happens

00:53:32.697 --> 00:53:35.513
"when you watch these other
programs for 22 minutes."

00:53:35.513 --> 00:53:37.765
In any given day in America.

00:53:37.765 --> 00:53:40.140
And at midpoint to CBS,

00:53:40.140 --> 00:53:42.609
not that any of these are faultless.

00:53:42.609 --> 00:53:45.114
These organizations screw up too.

00:53:45.114 --> 00:53:46.355
But for the most part,

00:53:46.355 --> 00:53:49.456
as this Wall Street
Journal article emphasizes,

00:53:49.456 --> 00:53:50.560
they're just talking

00:53:50.560 --> 00:53:54.633
about American stories across
the entirety of America.

00:53:56.017 --> 00:53:59.680
A business owner that
just lost everything.

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:02.424
Somebody whose father
just died of covid-19.

00:54:02.424 --> 00:54:05.775
Just American stories, and
they don't really mention Trump

00:54:05.775 --> 00:54:08.430
throughout any of this reporting.

00:54:08.430 --> 00:54:09.910
And I thought that's really interesting.

00:54:09.910 --> 00:54:11.810
Maybe, that's a sense of
perspective that I need,

00:54:11.810 --> 00:54:14.150
because maybe I don't see that as much,

00:54:14.150 --> 00:54:16.550
because I live in this
sort of political bubble.

00:54:19.948 --> 00:54:22.170
And I think what we need

00:54:22.170 --> 00:54:25.791
in America is better
definitions and clear lines

00:54:25.791 --> 00:54:28.258
between left and right-wing media,

00:54:28.258 --> 00:54:31.716
and between opinion journalism and news.

00:54:31.716 --> 00:54:35.171
And if we can collectively
educate ourselves

00:54:35.171 --> 00:54:36.291
on the differences,

00:54:36.291 --> 00:54:39.434
and if journalists can
be responsible enough

00:54:39.434 --> 00:54:43.340
to kinda self-report what
they're actually doing

00:54:43.340 --> 00:54:46.050
and who they're actually representing,

00:54:46.050 --> 00:54:48.172
I think that might go a long way.

00:54:48.172 --> 00:54:50.880
- I think you're probably
a little more hopeful

00:54:50.880 --> 00:54:53.280
for some sort of resurrection than I am.

00:54:53.280 --> 00:54:54.447
I'm also worried just

00:54:54.447 --> 00:54:58.980
because young people just don't
watch the NBC Nightly News,

00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:00.957
or they ABC Nightly News,
or CBS Nightly News,

00:55:00.957 --> 00:55:03.020
so they might be more fair.

00:55:03.020 --> 00:55:04.120
I'm actually not saying they aren't,

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:06.279
but I just don't know any 22 year old,

00:55:06.279 --> 00:55:08.487
if I was to poll at any
event that I went to,

00:55:08.487 --> 00:55:11.828
who's watching Lester Holt on
NBC, I just don't think it--

00:55:11.828 --> 00:55:14.230
- They're not watching Fox either.

00:55:14.230 --> 00:55:16.529
Conservatives aren't
watching Fox or MSNBC.

00:55:16.529 --> 00:55:21.090
They're watching this show on YouTube,

00:55:21.090 --> 00:55:21.923
and they're watching,

00:55:21.923 --> 00:55:24.110
they're looking at my Instagram accounts.

00:55:24.110 --> 00:55:26.047
So, that goes back to
what I was saying before.

00:55:26.047 --> 00:55:28.561
You gotta meet everybody where they're at.

00:55:28.561 --> 00:55:29.682
- So, give me,

00:55:29.682 --> 00:55:33.710
I know no politician likes
being in the predication game,

00:55:33.710 --> 00:55:36.370
but sort of try to lay out
the next couple of months

00:55:36.370 --> 00:55:38.590
as we roll into this coming election.

00:55:38.590 --> 00:55:39.686
I think we still have an election coming.

00:55:39.686 --> 00:55:41.220
It hasn't been canceled yet,

00:55:41.220 --> 00:55:43.246
and God knows what's
gonna happen with Biden.

00:55:43.246 --> 00:55:45.207
Well, actually, let's talk
about that for a minute.

00:55:45.207 --> 00:55:47.631
What do you make of what has happened here

00:55:47.631 --> 00:55:49.991
with the Democrats and that, ultimately,

00:55:49.991 --> 00:55:52.830
everybody fell in line behind Biden.

00:55:52.830 --> 00:55:55.508
As we're taping this right
now Obama finally did endorse,

00:55:55.508 --> 00:55:57.466
way later than it seemed sensible.

00:55:57.466 --> 00:56:01.924
I mean, truly, do you think
Biden has the mental capacity

00:56:01.924 --> 00:56:03.190
to do this?

00:56:03.190 --> 00:56:05.462
And do you think the DNC
is just sitting there

00:56:05.462 --> 00:56:06.757
with some card in their back pocket

00:56:06.757 --> 00:56:11.060
that either is the VP
replaces him at the convention

00:56:11.060 --> 00:56:13.420
or right after, which would be the most,

00:56:13.420 --> 00:56:14.786
right after an inauguration, potentially,

00:56:14.786 --> 00:56:16.980
which would be the most perverse thing.

00:56:16.980 --> 00:56:20.440
But at this point, I feel
like all rules are off.

00:56:20.440 --> 00:56:22.960
That sort of where I'm at
with them at this point.

00:56:22.960 --> 00:56:25.967
- Well, the second question
is fascinating (laughs).

00:56:27.168 --> 00:56:30.820
I would've loved to pontificated,
but I just have no idea.

00:56:30.820 --> 00:56:34.807
And if I were them, I would be
thinking of a way to do that.

00:56:34.807 --> 00:56:36.130
'Cause, just to answer your
- I mean it's just the

00:56:36.130 --> 00:56:37.262
sad truth, right?

00:56:37.262 --> 00:56:38.709
- Yeah, 'cause to answer
your first question,

00:56:38.709 --> 00:56:40.590
there's been a very clear decline

00:56:40.590 --> 00:56:42.423
and it happened very recently.

00:56:43.872 --> 00:56:45.630
When you have no more pressure on you,

00:56:45.630 --> 00:56:47.550
all you have to do is go
down to your game room,

00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:49.500
or study, or whatever he's filming in,

00:56:49.500 --> 00:56:54.500
and simply look like a
leader with no responsibility

00:56:56.610 --> 00:56:58.203
on your shoulders at all.

00:56:59.450 --> 00:57:02.434
I can't imagine an easier thing to do.

00:57:02.434 --> 00:57:03.640
But he can't do it,

00:57:03.640 --> 00:57:05.580
and he's not making any
sense when he's speaking

00:57:05.580 --> 00:57:07.400
about things that he
should really understand

00:57:07.400 --> 00:57:09.922
and should be able to talk about.

00:57:09.922 --> 00:57:13.184
Add to that, the very
disingenuous criticism

00:57:13.184 --> 00:57:14.760
that he's often leveling,

00:57:14.760 --> 00:57:19.410
are usually just things that
he says he would do better,

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:21.224
but they're things the
administration is already doing.

00:57:21.224 --> 00:57:23.095
That's a more substantive argument,

00:57:23.095 --> 00:57:26.313
but as for the decline,
it's very clearly happening.

00:57:26.313 --> 00:57:29.398
And they can't ignore that.

00:57:29.398 --> 00:57:32.869
That being said, if they
were to try to disrupt

00:57:32.869 --> 00:57:37.869
that at the convention,
I mean, if I'm them.

00:57:38.176 --> 00:57:41.260
If I'm a fly on their wall
in their strategy session,

00:57:41.260 --> 00:57:43.445
I'm sure they're weighing that option.

00:57:43.445 --> 00:57:45.418
But the problem is if they do that,

00:57:45.418 --> 00:57:49.036
how does that other person
gain any momentum in time

00:57:49.036 --> 00:57:50.810
to actually win the election?

00:57:50.810 --> 00:57:52.718
And the question of are we
still gonna have elections?

00:57:52.718 --> 00:57:55.530
Yes, I believe we will.

00:57:55.530 --> 00:57:57.210
I mean, we should definitely be prepared

00:57:57.210 --> 00:57:59.864
for another uptake in cases in November,

00:57:59.864 --> 00:58:01.690
because it will be fall.

00:58:01.690 --> 00:58:05.583
But I think we'll be well-prepared for it.

00:58:05.583 --> 00:58:09.498
And I hope that American
resilience shows through.

00:58:09.498 --> 00:58:11.692
